---
/**
 * /bid/-buyers
 *
 * ── THE APPROVED DESIGN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Built on the same template as /bid/: P1 "Obligation ledger", Figma
 * `223:3` on page `Page — Product (Velstrian Buy)` (`223:2`), with P2's hero graphic
 * (`223:5`) (three occluding rotated planes), and the motion board
 * `Motion — Product page` `243:2`, frame `243:3`.
 *
 * The two pages are one product sold to two sides of the same table, so they
 * take the same surfaces, the same rhythm and the same three cited figures. The
 * plane stack carries s.52, s.71 and PPN 026 on both, because those are the
 * duties the supplier prices against and the authority publishes against: the
 * same three instruments read from opposite ends.
 *
 * WHAT IS DIFFERENT HERE, AND IT IS THE ONE THING THAT MATTERS ON THIS PAGE:
 * the gate artefact under "The AI reads. It never scores." draws the boundary.
 * Three things the pre-read does cross the line; setting a mark stops dead at
 * it. That is the same M-P3 sequence /bid/ spends on a refused figure, and
 * it argues the identical point (the engine declines what it must not do)
 * from the buying side.
 *
 * REUSED, NOT REBUILT: Section.astro, `.surface`, `.sv-raised`, `.sv-floor`,
 * `.sv-numeral`, `.sv-spot`, `.sv-grain`, `[data-field]` and `[data-lit]`, and
 * every timing from --m-beat, --m-cycle and the three --m-ease-* curves. The
 * one thing NOT reused is src/scripts/motion.ts, and the reason is the payload
 * budget rather than the architecture: see the note on the inline trigger at
 * the foot of this file.
 *
 * ── OA-15 IS RESOLVED HERE, which is why this page waited for the port ──────
 *
 * The legacy page uses "TOMs theme" seven times with no qualification anywhere,
 * while /bid/ is careful about exactly this claim: "aligned to National TOMs
 * conventions" and "not a full National TOMs implementation". Two sibling pages
 * describing one engine, one disclaiming a framework claim and one not. A
 * contracting authority reading the buyer page could reasonably infer a full
 * National TOMs implementation, and this is the same pattern class as the
 * framework that had to be stripped from 54 places.
 *
 * I did not patch the legacy page because it carries an FAQPage JSON-LD block
 * mirroring its visible answers; changing one without the other recreates the
 * drift removed from /faq. Here both come from FAQS below, so the hedge lands in
 * the visible answer AND the structured data in one edit.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED, PRECISELY: the acronym is expanded to "National TOMs" on first
 * use, and FAQ 3 carries /bid/'s ALREADY-PUBLISHED sentence, "It is not a
 * full National TOMs implementation." No new claim is made. A claim is
 * narrowed, using wording that is already live on a sibling page. Confirmation
 * that this is accurate for the buyer side is still logged as OA-15.
 *
 * THE SPECIMEN IS AN ILLUSTRATION, NOT A SCREENSHOT, and says so three times:
 * in the standfirst, in the figure's accessible name, and in the visible
 * caption. That is the pattern the rest of the site should follow. This page
 * got it right where /bid/'s earlier social value figure got it wrong.
 *
 * There is no product screenshot on this page and that is deliberate: the
 * capture harness cannot authenticate against these surfaces, so every attempt
 * renders "Your session has expired". A drawing labelled as a drawing beats a
 * screenshot of an error.
 *
 * WIRE VALUE: ?product=buyer-side is read by the contact page to preselect the
 * subject. It differs from /bid/'s ?enquiry=limited-access. Preserve both.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/ui/Carousel.astro';
/* A-45. The hero proof card is ONE artefact appearing on two routes. Its markup
   is DutyCard.astro, its recipe is styles/duty-card.css (loaded by Base.astro),
   and its figures and citations are data/duty.ts. Only the wording stays here,
   because this page addresses the party that HOLDS the duty and /bid/ the
   party it is imposed on. Until 2026-08-04 this file carried a hand-copy of the
   entire recipe and every correction had to be made in both places. */
import DutyCard from '../components/sections/DutyCard.astro';
import { S52, S71, PPN026 } from '../data/duty';
import { BUYER_SCREENS } from '../data/crowmark-screens';
import { BUYER_PLANS, startHref } from '../data/pricing';
import { faqPage, breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Velstrian Buy | UK social value oversight | Velstrian';
const description =
  'Publish a social value requirement with a deterministic rubric, locate evidence in bids verbatim and track delivery after award. Your panel scores, not the AI.';

const DEMO = '/contact/?product=buyer-side#contact-form';

/*
  ── "REQUEST A DEMO", NOT "BOOK A DEMO", A-117, 2026-09-01 ──────────────────

  This constant is a CONTACT FORM. It is not a calendar. The three controls it
  drives on this page said "Book a demo", which is the label the site uses for
  the Calendly 30 minute booking on /bid/, /sectors, /tools and
  layouts/Sector.astro. Counted across the tree: eight "Book a demo" controls,
  four opening Calendly and four opening a form. One label, two behaviours.

  A-117 REMOVED EXACTLY THIS DEFECT FROM THE HOMEPAGE and wrote the reason
  down at pages/index.astro: the deleted button "said Book a demo and went to
  the contact form, not to the Calendly booking that the same label opens on
  /bid/, /sectors and /tools, so it promised a booking and delivered a
  form". That pass fixed one instance of eight. These are the rest.

  THE DESTINATION DOES NOT MOVE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Pointing these four at
  Calendly instead would also make the label true, and it would change a
  conversion path on a P3 polish item, which is the specific thing the owner
  deferred this row for. Changing the WORD costs nothing and fixes the promise.
  "Request" is also the honest verb here and the one the rest of the site
  already uses, because a form is answered by a person rather than by a slot.

  The four Calendly controls keep "Book a demo". After this, the label predicts
  the behaviour everywhere.
*/

/*
 * THE HERO ARTEFACT: the same three duties /bid/ draws, read from the
 * authority's end. Every figure cited to a named instrument, because the
 * standing rule has two options and no third.
 *
 * s.52 SETS AND PUBLISHES. s.71 ASSESSES AND PUBLISHES. Two duties, twelve
 * months apart, and they must never be merged into one sentence.
 *
 * ONLY THE LABELS ARE HERE, and "you" in each of them is the contracting
 * authority. The figures and the citations are the instrument itself and come
 * from data/duty.ts, declared once for both routes. The three citation strings
 * on this page and on /bid/ were character-for-character identical, which is
 * two copies of the only kind of statement on this site that can be checked
 * against a published document.
 */
const DUTY = [
  { ...S52, label: 'KPIs you must publish' },
  { ...S71, label: 'months to your assessment' },
  { ...PPN026, label: 'minimum evaluation weighting' },
];

/*
 * THE BOUNDARY, DRAWN. Three things the pre-read does cross the line. The
 * fourth stops dead at it, and it stops because of a duty rather than because
 * of a setting: under the Procurement Act 2023 equal treatment duty the panel
 * evaluates and scores every response.
 *
 * The refused row carries the WORD as well as the colour, so the boundary
 * survives with every pixel of hue removed. WCAG 1.4.1.
 */
const BOUNDARY = [
  { name: 'Locate evidence per criterion', src: 'quoted verbatim from the response', ok: true },
  { name: 'Drop what it cannot find', src: 'no quote, no card', ok: true },
  { name: 'Flag uneven scoring', src: 'advisory, after your panel has scored', ok: true },
  { name: 'Set, change or suggest a mark', src: 'the panel evaluates and scores', ok: false },
];

const PROOF = [
  { lead: 'Never scores', label: 'The panel evaluates' },
  { lead: 'Verbatim', label: 'Quotes located, not written' },
  // Acronym expanded. The legacy strip read just "TOMs themes".
  { lead: 'National TOMs', label: 'Committed vs delivered, by theme' },
  { lead: 's.52 · s.71', label: 'Procurement Act 2023' },
];

const KEY_FACTS = [
  {
    term: 'What it is',
    detail:
      'Requirement building, evidence location and post-award social value oversight for the buying side of a competition.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Who it is for',
    detail:
      'UK contracting authorities, councils, NHS bodies and universities, and private sector procurement teams running a competition.',
  },
  {
    term: 'What it is not',
    detail:
      'Not an e-sourcing portal and not a system of record. It sits alongside the portal you already run your competition in.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Price',
    detail:
      /* D235 (2026-10-19): both Buyer prices are published. Read from
         data/pricing.ts, so this fact cannot disagree with /pricing. */
      `Buyer Pro is £${BUYER_PLANS[0].monthly} a month and Buyer Portfolio is £${BUYER_PLANS[1].monthly} a month, both 10% off when paid yearly. Supplier pricing is published in full on the pricing page.`,
  },
  {
    term: 'The differentiator',
    detail:
      'Evidence is located verbatim and dropped if it cannot be found in the response. Scoring stays with your panel, by design.',
  },
  {
    term: 'The statute',
    detail:
      'Procurement Act 2023, sections 52 and 71, plus PPN 026 social value. These checks are advisory and are not legal advice.',
  },
];

const STAGES = [
  {
    n: '01',
    heading: 'Fix the rubric before the responses arrive',
    body: 'Build a social value requirement pack with a deterministic rubric: the measures, the weighting and how each will be evidenced, settled in advance. The basis of assessment is written down before anyone has read a bid.',
    extra:
      'Requirement packs are reusable, so the same measure means the same thing across directorates and across competitions.',
    where: 'Requirements · requirement packs',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    heading: 'Read the responses against what you published',
    body: "Open an evaluation against a requirement pack and a tender reference. For each published criterion, the pre-read locates the passages in that supplier's response that speak to it, quoted verbatim and dropped if they cannot be located in the source text.",
    extra:
      'Your panel then scores. The pre-read is a reading aid and is labelled as one on every criterion. The consistency check that runs afterwards is advisory and never changes a number.',
    where: 'Evaluations · evaluation pre-read',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    heading: 'Hold the winner to what they promised',
    body: 'Record the awarded contract and its social value commitments, then track delivery against them. Committed against delivered rolls up across the awarded estate by National TOMs theme and is RAG rated, and under-delivery is raised as an alert rather than left to be noticed in a quarterly pack.',
    extra:
      'Suppliers carry a rolling delivery track record across your contracts, so repeated under-delivery is visible across the estate rather than only inside the one contract it happened on.',
    where: 'Contracts · Suppliers · Portfolio · oversight',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    heading: 'Produce the return, and the reports underneath it',
    body: 'An award notice return is assembled across your recorded contracts for you to review and publish, and the underlying reports export for a committee pack or a scrutiny request. The section 52 and section 71 checks are applied as advisory steps, and are not legal advice.',
    where: 'Transparency · Reports and exports',
  },
];

const SURFACES = [
  {
    heading: 'Social value oversight',
    body: 'Committed against delivered social value across your awarded contracts and suppliers, by National TOMs theme, with under delivery raised as an alert.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Requirements',
    body: 'Social value requirement packs. Define the measures and the weighting for a tender before responses arrive.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Evaluations',
    body: 'Score supplier bids against a requirement rubric. Scores are evaluator set. The AI consistency check is advisory only and never changes a number.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Evaluation pre-read',
    body: 'A reading aid that locates evidence in a response against each published criterion. It never scores.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Contracts',
    body: 'Awarded contracts and their social value commitments, recorded so delivery has something to be measured against.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Suppliers',
    body: 'Your supplier directory and rolling delivery track record across the contracts you have awarded.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Portfolio',
    body: 'Committed against delivered across all contracts, by theme, RAG rated on the bands below.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Transparency and reports',
    body: 'A Procurement Act 2023 award notice return across your contracts, plus exportable reports for committee and scrutiny.',
  },
  /* R285-WEB-CLAIMS-AUDIT-01 U3, M5 and M6. The heading said "Eight surfaces"
     while the workspace holds the screens below as well, each a route under
     crowagent-platform web/app/(dashboard)/buyer and described from its own
     copy catalogue in web/src/shared/i18n/messages/public-sector. */
  {
    heading: 'Supplier invitations and clarifications',
    body: 'Invite suppliers to respond by personal link. Each response gets a receipt, and the answer to a supplier question is published to every invited supplier without saying who asked.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Quick RFQ',
    body: 'For smaller requests: describe the job, check the scope, download an RFQ pack and send it to suppliers by personal link. Each supplier uploads a quote and gets a receipt, and the quote lands on the evaluation. You write the scope. The AI does not draft it.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Pipeline',
    body: 'Every procurement you are planning, running, evaluating, awarding or managing, with its dates and value, worked out from your own records each time you open it.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Supplier screening',
    body: 'Checked against public registers when a response arrives or a supplier joins an evaluation. It is not a score and it never excludes a supplier. You decide.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Supplier risk',
    body: 'Risk signals across your suppliers, with what the platform worked out from its own records kept apart from what your team has flagged.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Supplier relationship management',
    body: 'Scorecards, review sessions and joint actions per supplier, every open action in one table, and how your suppliers rate the working relationship, quarter by quarter.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Value realisation',
    body: 'Savings and other value claimed against each supplier, and what finance has signed off.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Whole life costs',
    body: 'Cost each way of delivering a requirement across its whole life, from assumptions you supply.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Bid normalisation and comparison',
    body: "Enter each supplier's price schedule to see every bid as one whole life figure over the same term and quantities, then set the options side by side and record what your team judges.",
  },
];

/** Each band carries a TEXT label as well as a colour: already correct on the
    legacy page, and preserved rather than "improved". */
/* R285-WEB-CLAIMS-AUDIT-01 U6. The thresholds were located in code:
   crowagent-platform web/src/features/public-sector/monitoring-logic.ts lines
   106 and 107 (95 and 80) and the legend in
   web/src/shared/i18n/messages/public-sector/portfolio.ts. Below 80% has two
   bands there, not one: "not yet due" while no deadline has passed, and
   "breached" after it. The page said "breached" for both. */
const BANDS = [
  { value: '≥ 95%', label: 'On track', tone: 'on' },
  { value: '80 to 94%', label: 'At risk', tone: 'risk' },
  { value: '< 80%, no deadline passed', label: 'Not yet due', tone: 'none' },
  { value: '< 80%, deadline passed', label: 'Breached', tone: 'breach' },
  { value: 'Nothing committed', label: 'No data, never green', tone: 'none' },
];

const DOES = [
  'Builds and publishes a social value requirement with a deterministic rubric',
  'Locates evidence per published criterion, quoted verbatim from the response',
  'Gives the panel one place to score against the rubric you published',
  'Invites suppliers to respond by personal link, with a receipt for every response and one clarification register for all of them',
  'Runs Quick RFQ for smaller requests: an RFQ pack sent by personal link and the quotes collected in one place',
  'Records awarded contracts and the commitments made in them',
  'Rolls committed against delivered up by National TOMs theme, and flags under delivery',
  'Assembles the Procurement Act 2023 award notice return for you to publish',
];

const DOES_NOT = [
  'Score, rank or recommend a mark on any response',
  'Replace Jaggaer, Atamis, In-tend, ProContract or Proactis as your system of record',
  'Publish your formal tender notices. Those go on Find a Tender or through your e-sourcing portal',
  'Hold the formal competition audit trail, which stays in your e-sourcing system',
  'Give legal advice. The statutory checks it applies are advisory',
  "Form or publish a view on a supplier's wider standing",
];

/* [R285-P2-DROP-01] The fifth line ("Invoice or PO billing, SSO and SAML, DPA and security
   review") described a scoped engagement. It left this list when Buyer Pro became a listed
   self serve price (D195), and it stays in QUALIFY below, where it is a reason to talk to us. */
const INCLUDED = [
  'Requirement builder with a deterministic social value rubric',
  'Evidence located per published criterion, quoted verbatim',
  'Evaluation workspace across directorates',
  'Delivery monitoring against what was promised',
];

/* [R285-P2-DROP-01] D195. The two Buyer offerings and their published figures come from
   data/pricing.ts, which holds them for /pricing too, so the two pages cannot disagree. */
const [BUYER_PRO, BUYER_PORTFOLIO] = BUYER_PLANS;
const proAnnual = BUYER_PRO.annual!.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const portfolioAnnual = BUYER_PORTFOLIO.annual!.toLocaleString('en-GB');

const QUALIFY = [
  'You run competitions and evaluate responses',
  'Social value is scored in your competitions and monitored after award',
  'You need multi entity or group consolidation',
  'You buy through a framework, or need a DPA or custom terms',
  'You have to publish a Procurement Act award notice return',
];

/** Feeds the visible accordion AND the FAQPage node. One source. */
const FAQS = [
  {
    question: 'Does the AI score or rank supplier bids?',
    answer:
      'No. Scores are evaluator set. The evaluation pre-read is a reading aid that locates evidence in a response against each published criterion. It does not score, rank, or recommend a mark, and every criterion card carries an advisory label. Under the Procurement Act 2023 equal treatment duty the panel evaluates and scores every response. There is also an AI consistency check on completed evaluations, which is advisory and never changes a number.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Velstrian Buy replace our e-sourcing portal?',
    answer:
      'No. It is not a system of record and it does not run your formal competition. Formal notices and the competition audit trail stay in the portal you already use, such as Jaggaer, Atamis, In-tend, ProContract or Proactis. It sits alongside that portal: you build the social value requirement, read the responses against it, and monitor delivery after award. For smaller requests, Quick RFQ sends the request to suppliers by personal link and collects their quotes and receipts in one place.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is social value handled on the buyer side?',
    /* THE HEDGE. Final sentence is /bid/'s already-published wording,
       applied here so the two pages describe one engine the same way. */
    answer:
      'You build a requirement pack with a deterministic rubric, so the weighting and the measures are fixed before responses arrive rather than argued afterwards. After award, commitments on each contract roll up against delivery by National TOMs theme and are RAG rated: on track at 95% or above, at risk from 80%, breached below 80%. An authority with nothing committed shows as no data rather than green. Measures are aligned to National TOMs conventions. It is not a full National TOMs implementation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are the quotes it shows us real, or generated?',
    answer:
      "Located, not generated. Every quote is found verbatim in the supplier's own submitted text and is dropped if it cannot be located there. The pre-read points at evidence in the response. It does not write a summary of what the response might have said.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does it cost?',
    answer:
      `Buyer Pro is £${BUYER_PRO.monthly} a month, or £${proAnnual} a year, with ${BUYER_PRO.seats} users, ${BUYER_PRO.credits.toLocaleString('en-GB')} AI credits a month and ${BUYER_PRO.guestEvaluators} guest evaluators. Buyer Portfolio is £${BUYER_PORTFOLIO.monthly} a month, or £${portfolioAnnual} a year, with ${BUYER_PORTFOLIO.seats} users, ${BUYER_PORTFOLIO.credits.toLocaleString('en-GB')} AI credits a month and ${BUYER_PORTFOLIO.guestEvaluators} guest evaluators. Annual prices are 10% off twelve months. Pay by card, or pay for Buyer Portfolio by invoice on 30 days with a PO number.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Can it produce our Procurement Act transparency return?',
    answer:
      'It produces an award notice return across your recorded contracts, which you review and publish. The statutory checks it applies, including section 52 on published KPIs above an estimated value of £5m and section 71 on assessing and publishing performance, are advisory and are not legal advice.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  // WebPage. Generic, but it was published on the legacy page and a parity
  // run flags anything that silently stops shipping (OA-24).
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: title,
    description,
    url: `${SITE.origin}/buy`,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Velstrian', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
  },
  ...faqPage(FAQS),
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Velstrian Buy', path: '/buy/' },
  ]),
];

/*
 * WHEN THIS PAGE LAST MATERIALLY CHANGED. Read by Seo.astro into a WebPage
 * node's dateModified, and from there by scripts/build-sitemap.js into
 * <lastmod>. It is a FACT about this page, not a build timestamp: bump it only
 * when the page's content actually changes, never on a redeploy. Leaving it
 * stale is safer than advancing it falsely, because a sitemap that claims
 * everything changed is discounted.
 */
const UPDATED = new Date('2026-08-05');

---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/buy" schema={schema} updatedAt={UPDATED}>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="For buyers and contracting authorities"
    title="You publish the requirement. We locate the evidence."
    standfirst="Evidence for each published criterion, quoted word for word from the response or left out. Your panel scores, across public authorities and private sector procurement."
    id="hero"
  >
    <div class="cw cw--hero" data-spot="off">
      <div class="btn-row">
        <Button href={DEMO}>Request a demo</Button>
        <Button href="/bid/" variant="secondary">Bidding instead? See the supplier side</Button>
      </div>

      {/*
        ── THE PLANE STACK, `223:5` ──────────────────────────────────────────
        The drawing is DutyCard.astro and the recipe is styles/duty-card.css.
        The geometry, the tilt and the arithmetic that gates it are documented
        there, beside the declarations they govern, rather than restated here.
        This page carried a second copy of that reasoning as well as a second
        copy of the CSS. What is left below is the authority's wording, which is
        the only part of the artefact that is this route's.
      */}
      <DutyCard
        kicker="What you publish against"
        rows={DUTY}
        barLabel="Award evaluation, one competition"
        legendNote="the remaining weighting is yours to set"
      />

      <ul class="proof" role="list">
        {PROOF.map((p) => <li><strong>{p.lead}</strong> {p.label}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The workspace"
    title="One workspace, one thread."
    standfirst="The main screens in the buyer workspace, what each one is for, and what each one looks like."
    id="workspace"
  >
    <ul class="grid grid--2">
      {SURFACES.map((s) => (
        <li class="card surface"><h3 class="card-h card__h">{s.heading}</h3><p class="card__body">{s.body}</p></li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    {/* ══ THE BUYER SCREENS, ADDED 2026-08-04 ═══════════════════════════════
     *
     * Owner instruction: "crowmark supplier and buyer must use created poducts
     * screens in figma created products in carousels". This is the same
     * ui/Carousel.astro the supplier page runs, with the buyer set from
     * data/bid/-screens.ts; there is one carousel component on this site
     * and this does not add a second.
     *
     * INSIDE THIS SECTION RATHER THAN AFTER IT, AND THAT IS A LAYOUT DECISION
     * WITH A REASON. The list above names the eight buyer surfaces in words;
     * SIX of the eight are drawn below. The two that are not are the phone
     * renders that came out of the set on 2026-08-31, because this carousel
     * draws a desktop browser window and a phone screen inside one is wrong at
     * any size: see data/bid/-screens.ts. Pairing them under one heading is the
     * stronger argument, and it also avoids the cost /bid/ paid when its
     * showcase was inserted mid-page: `sv-floor` alternates section by section,
     * so a new section here would flip the floor on all five sections below it,
     * including a closing CTA that carries its own ambient field.
     *
     * WHAT THE CAPTIONS AND ALT TEXT MAY AND MAY NOT SAY is written out in
     * data/bid/-screens.ts. In short: they describe what each screen shows,
     * they never claim it is the live product or an account, and they do not
     * carry an inline "illustrative" or "sample data" label either, because the
     * owner calls the data question out separately.
     * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    {/* Wrapped rather than given a class prop. A class handed to a child
        component's root cannot be reached by this page's scoped styles: Astro
        stamps the component's OWN data-astro-cid on its root and not the
        parent's, so `.workspace__screens` here would compile to
        `.workspace__screens[data-astro-cid-BUYERS]` and match nothing. Measured
        in this repo; the same note is in components/blog/PostImage.astro. */}
    <div class="workspace__screens">
      <Carousel slides={BUYER_SCREENS} path="buyer" label="Velstrian Buy screens" />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The line we do not cross"
    title="The AI reads. It never scores."
    id="boundary"
  >
    <p class="lead">
      Under the Procurement Act 2023 equal treatment duty, the panel evaluates and scores every
      response. So the pre-read locates evidence and stops. It sets no mark, changes no mark and
      suggests no mark, and every criterion it reports carries an advisory label in the
      interface, not in a footnote.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      There is a second AI step, a consistency check that runs over a completed evaluation and
      flags where scoring looks uneven. It is advisory in the same way. It never changes a
      number.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      This is a design constraint rather than a setting. There is no mode in which the software
      marks a bid for you.
    </p>

    {/*
      ── THE BOUNDARY, DRAWN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Three tracks cross the line. The fourth stops at it and carries a drawn
      cross. Cover every word of the prose above and this still says what the
      section says, which is the test the design standard sets.
    */}
    <div class="cw" data-lit>
